## Elongated vs. Square Radiant Cut in Yellow Gold

The L/W ratio determines how your ring looks on your hand. And for yellow gold specifically, the elongated radiant has a specific advantage.

An elongated radiant (L/W ratio 1.20+) reads rectangular. On a finger it looks like a miniature emerald cut — long, elegant, and finger-lengthening. The extended length means more facets are catching the yellow gold's reflected warm light.

Many buyers find the elongated radiant in yellow gold the more dramatic of the two shapes.

A square radiant (L/W ratio below 1.10) reads compact and bold — closer to a cushion or princess in visual footprint. It makes a strong, confident statement. Square radiant cut buyers who want more sparkle than a cushion provides consistently choose this combination.

Both shapes are excellent in yellow gold. The choice is personal style.

See the full shape guides at [Elongated Radiant Cut Diamond](/elongated-radiant-cut-diamond) and [Square Radiant Cut Diamond](/square-radiant-cut-diamond).

---

> "Yellow gold and radiant cut is one of my favourite pairings to recommend. The 70-facet fire in warm afternoon light is something you have to see in person to believe. Buyers come in wanting white gold. I show them the same stone in yellow gold first. Most change their minds on the spot. It is not just sparkle — it is warm fire. Coloured, directional fire. And you save $700–$2,000 on colour in the process." — Farzana Hasan, **[Diamond Critics](/about-farzana/)**

